-- title:
--   Surjectivity of the degree-$D$ Macaulay matrix characterizes ideal membership in degree $D$
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, $\sigma$ a finite set of variables and $\iota$ a finite index set. For $d\in\mathbb{Z}$ let $\mathcal{M}_d=\{s\in\mathbb{N}^\sigma : |s|=d\}$ be the finite set of exponent vectors of total degree $d$ (empty if $d<0$), and write $X^s=\prod_jX_j^{s_j}$. Let $F=(F_i)_{i\in\iota}$ be polynomials in $K[X_j : j\in\sigma]$ with $F_i$ homogeneous of degree $e_i\in\mathbb{Z}$, and let $D\in\mathbb{Z}$. The degree-$D$ Macaulay matrix $M_D$ of $F$ has rows indexed by $s\in\mathcal{M}_D$, columns indexed by pairs $(i,t)$ with $i\in\iota$ and $t\in\mathcal{M}_{D-e_i}$, and entries
--   $$
--   M_D[s,(i,t)]=\text{the coefficient of } X^s \text{ in } F_i\,X^t .
--   $$
--   Then the linear map $b\mapsto M_Db$ (from vectors indexed by columns to vectors indexed by rows) is surjective if and only if every homogeneous polynomial of degree $D$ lies in the ideal generated by the $F_i$:
--   $$
--   M_D\ \text{surjective}\iff\Big(\forall f\in K[X_j : j\in\sigma]:\ f\ \text{homogeneous of degree } D\ \Longrightarrow\ f\in(F_i : i\in\iota)\Big).
--   $$
--
--   The columns of $M_D$ are the coefficient vectors of the products $F_iX^t$, which span the degree-$D$ part of the ideal $(F_i)$. The theorem therefore turns ideal membership in one degree into a rank condition on a matrix whose entries depend linearly on the coefficients of the $F_i$. This is what allows such certificates to be specialized and deformed.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Degrees are integers and homogeneity is `IsWeightedHomogeneous` with the constant weight $1$; $\mathcal{M}_d$ is `DegreeIndex σ d`, and surjectivity refers to `(macaulayMatrix e F D).mulVec`.
